WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.080
If you recall the tragedy of Heath&nbsp;
Ledger and his postumous Oscar win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.160
you might be surprised to learn he wasn’t&nbsp;
the first to win the award after he died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.720
A few other actors have also given Academy Award&nbsp;
winning performances and then passed away shortly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.560
after. All these actors are recalled for&nbsp;
their great, award winning performances.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.840
In a career spanning decades, Henry Fonda left an&nbsp;
indelible mark on the history of American cinema&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.480
as one of the most identifiable movie stars of his&nbsp;
generation. An actor known for taking roles that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:31.440
emphasized his own American ideals, Fonda compiled&nbsp;
a robust collection of credits. His best-known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.000
films include Young Mr. Lincoln, The Grapes of&nbsp;
Wrath, and 12 Angry Men. He also launched the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.720
Fonda acting dynasty, which includes his children&nbsp;
Jane and Peter and his granddaughter Bridget.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.200
Despite his many acting achievements, much of&nbsp;
Fonda's career went unnoticed by the Academy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.880
He was nominated for Best Actor for The Grapes of&nbsp;
Wrath in 1941, and nominated as a producer for 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.880
Angry Men in 1958, but as Fonda entered his 70s,&nbsp;
he had yet to win an Oscar. That changed with On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.280
Golden Pond, in which he co-starred with fellow&nbsp;
screen legend Katharine Hepburn as an aging couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.240
trying to mend family fences. The film earned&nbsp;
Fonda and Hepburn Best Actor and Best Actress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.200
Oscars, respectively, giving Fonda his first&nbsp;
and only Oscar win. He died of heart failure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:17.120
just a few months later, in August of 1982, at&nbsp;
the age of 77. Hepburn acted only sporadically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.920
after On Golden Pond and lived largely in&nbsp;
quiet retirement until her death in 2003.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.280
After beginning his career&nbsp;
on the stage and in radio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.040
Peter Finch built a reputation as a&nbsp;
powerful acting presence on the big screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.120
and by the 1950s he was off and running,&nbsp;
earning international acclaim for films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.280
like A Town Like Alice, The Nun's Story, and&nbsp;
The Trials of Oscar Wilde. Today, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.160
Finch is best remembered for his role as&nbsp;
Howard Beale in the 1976 classic Network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.640
Beale, a devoted newsman who grows fed up with his&nbsp;
network's efforts to put profit over journalistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.600
integrity, allowed Finch to exercise every ounce&nbsp;
of fiery energy he had. His delivery of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.800
particular line cemented his place in countless&nbsp;
montages of the greatest movie lines of all time.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.480
"I'm as mad as hell, and I'm&nbsp;
not going to take this anymore."

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.080
Network was one of the most acclaimed films&nbsp;
of 1976, and it earned Finch his second Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.680
nomination. Sadly, in January of 1977, the day&nbsp;
after he'd appeared on The Tonight Show to promote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.320
the film, Finch died after suffering a heart&nbsp;
attack in the lobby of the Beverly Hills Hotel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.520
He was 60. In the weeks after he&nbsp;
died, his performance in Network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.160
earned him his first Globe, his fifth BAFTA&nbsp;
Award, and his first Oscar for Best Actor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.840
At the time of the award, he was the first&nbsp;
actor to ever win a posthumous Oscar.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.560
In a career spanning five decades, Melvyn&nbsp;
Douglas did it all, and to prove it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.000
he remains one of only two dozen people&nbsp;
to ever complete the Triple Crown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.440
of Acting. He won an Emmy, Tony, and Oscars&nbsp;
for his performances across stage and screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.400
Douglas began appearing in feature films in&nbsp;
1931 and quickly established himself as a suave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.000
quick-witted presence in everything&nbsp;
from The Old Dark House to Ninotchka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.640
which remains one of his best-known roles today.&nbsp;
He worked regularly in features throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.160
1930s and 1940s, and in the 1950s made the&nbsp;
successful leap to prolific television work.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.960
In 1964, amid a return to making movies,&nbsp;
Douglas won the Oscar for Best Supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.320
Actor for his role as Homer Bannon in Hud. That&nbsp;
success led to prolific work well into his 70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.360
as he continued to jump from big screen to small&nbsp;
and back again. In 1980, Douglas won his second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.160
Best Supporting Actor Oscar, this time for his&nbsp;
role as Benjamin Rand in Being There. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.680
Douglas passed away at the age of 80 in August of&nbsp;
1981, just over a year after his second Oscar win.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.600
Some actors spend their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.800
entire careers sitting in the shadow&nbsp;
of the Oscars, almost touching that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.920
golden statue but never quite grasping it,&nbsp;
despite decades of tantalizing near misses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.800
That was the case for Geraldine Page. An actress&nbsp;
who began building a name for herself on the stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.200
in the 1940s, Page gained Academy attention&nbsp;
at the very beginning of her film career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.840
earning a Best Supporting Actress nomination&nbsp;
for her role opposite John Wayne in Hondo.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.040
Another six nominations followed with no award,&nbsp;
including Best Actress for Summer and Smoke, Sweet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:00.000
Bird of Youth, and Interiors, and Best Supporting&nbsp;
Actress for You're a Big Boy Now, Pete 'n' Tillie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.800
and The Pope of Greenwich Village. Then, at&nbsp;
last, came Page's part in The Trip to Bountiful.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.960
Page's performance as an aging woman who wants&nbsp;
nothing more than to return to her rural home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.120
after years away earned her some of the best&nbsp;
reviews of her career. She received Golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.400
Globe and BAFTA nominations, and she finally&nbsp;
earned the Oscar for Best Actress in 1986.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:22.320
Sadly, her post-Oscar career&nbsp;
was short-lived. On June 13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.840
1987, Page was found dead in her apartment at&nbsp;
the age of 62 after an apparent heart attack.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.160
For actors of all ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.360
an Academy Award is supposed to be&nbsp;
a gateway to a wider world of acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.200
options. It's a rocket you can ride&nbsp;
to whatever opportunities you choose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.960
That should have been the case for Marie Dressler,&nbsp;
and even though she didn't die immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.680
after winning her Oscar, what happened in the&nbsp;
aftermath did cut her time tragically short.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.680
Like many performers during this period of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.960
the changing Hollywood landscape&nbsp;
proved challenging for Dressler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.880
She spent much of the 1920s without&nbsp;
being able to book a single film role.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.960
Finally, after struggling financially for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.360
the late 1920s and the introduction of sound&nbsp;
brought about a career renaissance for her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.840
In 1932, Dressler won the Academy Award&nbsp;
for Best Actress for Min and Bill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:10.400
a victory that seemed primed to catapult her to&nbsp;
even greater stardom. She launched into more major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:16.240
films, including Emma and Dinner at Eight, but by&nbsp;
1932 she'd received a critical cancer diagnosis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.600
Her health slowed her work to a crawl, and&nbsp;
she died in July of 1934 at the age of 65.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.040
We can trace one of the great filmmaking dynasties&nbsp;
in all of Hollywood back to Walter Huston.&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.320
He spent two decades working steadily&nbsp;
as a character actor and starting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.840
Huston line of family filmmakers.&nbsp;
It's perhaps only fitting, then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.840
that the film for which Huston finally won&nbsp;
his Oscar ended up being a family affair.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.320
In The Treasure of the Sierra Madre, written&nbsp;
and directed by his son, Huston played Howard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.720
an old-time gold prospector who leads two&nbsp;
younger collaborators in a search for treasure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.080
Walter Huston won the Oscar for Best&nbsp;
Supporting Actor for his performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.920
as Howard. His son John won the only&nbsp;
two Oscars of his long career — for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.280
Best Director and Best Adapted&nbsp;
Screenplay — for the film. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:03.040
Walter Huston completed just three more films&nbsp;
before he died in April of 1950 at the age of 67.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.240
Few actors in Hollywood's silent era were lucky&nbsp;
to survive the paradigm-shifting transition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.840
"talkies" at all, but a lucky few&nbsp;
were able to thrive in both eras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:15.760
Alice Brady was one of them, against&nbsp;
all odds. Born into a theater family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:20.720
the actress began her career on the stage before&nbsp;
starting to build her silent film resume in 1914.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.720
She worked steadily for several years before&nbsp;
leaving the screen to focus on her stage work.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.120
By the time she returned to the movies in&nbsp;
1933, Hollywood had changed dramatically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.960
but Brady's talent was still there. She&nbsp;
picked up right where she had left off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.560
launching a career in the sound era&nbsp;
that included roles in The Gay Divorcee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:42.720
Gold Diggers of 1935, and My Man Godfrey, for&nbsp;
which she was nominated for an Oscar in 1937.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.240
Just a year later, in 1938, Brady received her&nbsp;
second Oscar nomination for In Old Chicago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:51.040
but the afterglow was to be short-lived. Brady was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:55.760
diagnosed with cancer and died in&nbsp;
October of 1939 at the age of 46.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.120
Even before his big Oscar buzz, Heath Ledger was&nbsp;
widely recognized as one of the most promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.920
young actors of his generation. His career&nbsp;
launched on Australian television in the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.600
and he was an instant success when he began&nbsp;
appearing in American films like 10 Things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.320
I Hate About You and The Patriot. By the&nbsp;
mid-2000s, he was a full-fledged movie star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.880
and in 2005 alone he appeared in Casanova,&nbsp;
The Brothers Grimm, Lords of Dogtown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:22.720
and Brokeback Mountain. He received Golden&nbsp;
Globe, BAFTA, and Oscar nominations for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.640
performance in the last film, making him one&nbsp;
of the youngest Oscar nominees in history.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.160
Then came The Dark Knight. Hot&nbsp;
off the success of Batman Begins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.680
director Christopher Nolan cast Ledger&nbsp;
as his incarnation of The Joker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.600
While fan skepticism was initially high,&nbsp;
buzz from the set and early looks at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:43.840
character quickly built excitement for a&nbsp;
potentially iconic piece of superhero work.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:45.840
“haha”

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.120
Sadly, Ledger did not live to see&nbsp;
the full response to the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.160
He died in January of 2008 at the age of&nbsp;
just 28, after an accidental overdose in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.160
his New York City apartment, months before&nbsp;
The Dark Knight was set to premiere. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.400
the film finally hit theaters Ledger received&nbsp;
near-universal acclaim for his performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.560
and earned posthumous BAFTA, Golden Globe,&nbsp;
and Oscar wins for Best Supporting Actor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.950
That made him only the second actor ever&nbsp;
in history to win an Oscar after his death.

